From u7e0eb6a1dcbfe39c at groups.google.com  Tue Sep  9 12:58:29 2014
From: u7e0eb6a1dcbfe39c at groups.google.com (Matteo De Felice)
Date: Tue, 09 Sep 2014 12:58:29 +0000
Subject: aaply vs apply and the dimensions order
Message-ID: <CaYMSPVpUAo.PNEb66JnhHkJ@manipulatr.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/manipulatr/c/CaYMSPVpUAo/m/PNEb66JnhHkJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hi,
on the apply documentation we can read:

This function is very similar to apply <http://inside-r.org/r-doc/base/apply>, except that it will always return an array, and when the function returns >1 d data structures, those dimensions are added on to the highest dimensions, rather than the lowest dimensions.

I don't understand why. I mean, I wanted to replace my apply with aaply but now I would deal with the change in the order of the dimensions. I apply a sort of aggregation with a tapply on the first dimension of a 3d array passing from the dimensions c(1000, 20, 30) to c(100, 20, 30). If I replace the `apply` with `aaply` I finally get an array with the dimensions c(20, 30, 100). It will be easy to fix it but I am curious on the reasons of this behaviour.

Thanks again and thanks to plyr that is making my job easier and more entertaining,

Matteo

